  

<h2 id="plain-text-tasks-pencil-notes-digital-caveman-ism-and-some-obtf-love">Plain text tasks, pencil notes, digital caveman-ism, and some <span class="small-caps">OBTF</span> love</h2>
<p>I’m enjoying a bounty of family life at the moment! It truly is a delight. It’s also a time when most of the spare spaces of my calendar and brain are comfortably full.</p>
<p>So this week I will share with you a collection of things that I believe to be worth your time and mine. They are things I intend to explore more fully when the flow of my life becomes a little slower than at present.</p>
<ul>
<li><p>A great way to <a href="https://frantic.im/project-todo/" target="_blank">track todo items for projects</a>. Post discovered thanks to Endre on DFOS/Plain Text (<a href="https://app.dfos.com/j/877ve24zr73rcv7t9t8ckr" target="_blank">join here</a> if you’re not a member yet).</p></li>
<li><p>In this episode of <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SfY2JACiDGU&amp;t=544s" target="_blank">The Rest is Science</a>, Michael explains why he’s switched to a mechanical pencil for notes. Now while I’m in almost zero danger of having an energy drink explode all over my notebooks, he makes some great points.</p></li>
<li><p>I really appreciate Vlad Campos’ enthusiasm for mindful digital practices. Here’s his micro post on being a <a href="https://vladcampos.com/en/dcm/" target="_blank">digital caveman</a>, and here’s another on implementing his <a href="https://vladcampos.com/2026/07/02/the-timeline-system-on-paper.html" target="_blank">Timeline system on paper</a>. (See also <a href="https://medium.com/@vladcampos/the-tlsp-sidebars-are-full-of-surprises-i-didnt-see-this-coming-ace30e8c22a4" target="_blank">this follow-up post</a> on Medium)</p></li>
<li><p>Sara Schlijper runs through how she is using her <span class="small-caps">OBTF</span>. It’s in Dutch, so if that’s not a language you can read you’ll need to post this link <a href="https://saraschlijper.nl/obtf/" class="uri" target="_blank">https://saraschlijper.nl/obtf/</a> into <a href="https://translate.google.com/?sl=gl&amp;tl=en&amp;op=websites" target="_blank">Google Translate</a> or similar.</p></li>
</ul>
<p><span class="small-caps">PS</span>. Inspired by Johnny Decimal, I’m trying out Sentence case (as opposed to Title Case) for my article headers. It’s less<span class="push-single"></span> <span class="pull-single">‘</span>shouty’, as Johnny puts it, but I’m struggling not to see this kind of header as leaving the house only partly dressed! What do you think? Is Title Case a must for articles like this one?</p>
